## Why This Is Asked
This question tests mastery of maintaining order statistics with dynamic updates, handling duplicates efficiently, and implementing lazy deletion—all while achieving O(log n) time complexity.

## Key Concepts
- **Two heaps for median maintenance**: A max-heap stores the lower half, while a min-heap stores the upper half, allowing O(1) median retrieval
- **Duplicate handling**: Store actual values in heaps with a frequency counter map to track occurrences
- **Lazy deletion**: Defer removals by updating a delayed-delete map; prune elements lazily when they surface at heap tops to achieve O(log n) amortized erase

## Code Example
```python
import heapq

class DualHeap:
    def __init__(self):
        self.small = []  # max-heap (store negatives)
        self.large = []  # min-heap
        self.delayed = {}  # lazy deletion map
        self.small_size = 0
        self.large_size = 0

    def prune(self, heap):
        while heap:
            num = -heap[0] if heap is self.small else heap[0]
            if self.delayed.get(num, 0) > 0:
                heapq.heappop(heap)
                self.delayed[num] -= 1
                if self.delayed[num] == 0:
                    del self.delayed[num]
            else:
                break

    def rebalance(self):
        if self.small_size > self.large_size + 1:
            val = -heapq.heappop(self.small)
            self.small_size -= 1
            heapq.heappush(self.large, val)
            self.large_size += 1
        elif self.large_size > self.small_size + 1:
            val = heapq.heappop(self.large)
            self.large_size -= 1
            heapq.heappush(self.small, -val)
            self.small_size += 1

    def insert(self, num):
        if not self.small or num <= -self.small[0]:
            heapq.heappush(self.small, -num)
            self.small_size += 1
        else:
            heapq.heappush(self.large, num)
            self.large_size += 1
        self.rebalance()

    def erase(self, num):
        self.delayed[num] = self.delayed.get(num, 0) + 1
        if num <= -self.small[0]:
            self.small_size -= 1
        else:
            self.large_size -= 1
        self.rebalance()

    def getMedian(self):
        if self.small_size > self.large_size:
            self.prune(self.small)
            return -self.small[0]
        elif self.large_size > self.small_size:
            self.prune(self.large)
            return self.large[0]
        return (-self.small[0] + self.large[0]) / 2
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests mastery of persistent data structures, real-world versioning systems, and memory-time trade-offs. It also probes understanding of shared nodes and functional update semantics.

## Key Concepts
- Fully persistent data structures via path-copying
- Implicit treap or segment tree with augmented sums and sizes
- Version management with O(log n) mutations per update
- Correct boundary handling for empty intervals and edge operations

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(val, prio, left = null, right = null) {
    this.val = val;
    this.prio = prio;
    this.left = left;
    this.right = right;
    this.size = 1;
    this.sum = val;
  }

  update() {
    this.size = 1 + (this.left?.size ?? 0) + (this.right?.size ?? 0);
    this.sum = this.val + (this.left?.sum ?? 0) + (this.right?.sum ?? 0);
  }
}

function split(root, k) {
  if (!root) return [null, null];
  const leftSize = root.left?.size ?? 0;
  if (k <= leftSize) {
    const [l, r] = split(root.left, k);
    root.left = r;
    root.update();
    return [l, root];
  } else {
    const [l, r] = split(root.right, k - leftSize - 1);
    root.right = l;
    root.update();
    return [root, r];
  }
}

function merge(left, right) {
  if (!left || !right) return left ?? right;
  if (left.prio < right.prio) {
    left.right = merge(left.right, right);
    left.update();
    return left;
  } else {
    right.left = merge(left, right.left);
    right.update();
    return right;
  }
}

function rangeSum(root, l, r) {
  const [, mid] = split(root, l);
  const [target, rest] = split(mid, r - l + 1);
  const result = target?.sum ?? 0;
  return merge(merge(null, target), rest);
}

function insertAt(root, index, val) {
  const [left, right] = split(root, index);
  const newNode = new Node(val, Math.random());
  return merge(merge(left, newNode), right);
}

function eraseAt(root, index) {
  const [left, mid] = split(root, index);
  const [, right] = split(mid, 1);
  return merge(left, right);
}
```
